{"assets":[],"contestId":"740","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"740A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/740/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"740A - Alyona and copybooks"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - is the smallest non-negative integer such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a multiple of 4."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is 0, then, obviously, the answer is 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is equal to 1, then you can buy one set of one notebook, you can buy 3 sets of three notebooks, you can buy 1 set of three notebooks and 1 set of two notebooks. Take the most optimal response."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals 2, you can buy 2 sets of one notebook, 1 set of two notebooks, two sets of three notebooks. Take the most optimal response."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is equal to 3, you can buy 3 sets of one book, 1 set of single and 1 set of two notebooks, one set of three notebooks. Take the most optimal response."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"740B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/740/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"740B - Alyona and flowers"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If you restate the problem, it is clear that you need to take sub-arrays that have positive sum."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"739A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"739A - Alyona and mex"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Obviously, the answer to the problem can not be greater than the minimum length among the lengths of the sub-arrays. Suppose that the minimum length of all the sub-arrays is equal to len. Then the desired array is: 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ... "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"len"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1... . Not hard to make sure that mex of any subarray will be at least len."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"739B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"739B - Alyona and a tree"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's fix a vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". This node adds +1 to all the ancestors whose depth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"depth"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"depth"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"depth"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = the sum of the weights of edges on the path from the root to the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). It's a segment of the ancestors, ending in v, as the depth increases when moving to the leaves. It remains to find the first ancestor on the way up, it does not hold for him - so you can make a binary lifting or binary search, if you will be storing the path to the root in dfs. With the partial sums you can calculate the answer for each vertices."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"739C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"739C - Alyona and towers"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's consider the difference between two adjacent elements of array: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1] - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. Let us add "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the segment from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then it is clear that in the array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will change no more than two values ​​(at the ends of the addition of the segment), because if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1] + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1] - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"arr"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. Note that the answer is a sequence of positive elements of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + a sequence of negative elements of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (i.e., ... +1 +1 +1 +1 -1 -1 ..., as the mountain first increases and then decreases). To solve this problem it's enough to take the tree segments and store at each vertex response to the prefix, suffix, and the middle. Two segments [l; m] and [m + 1; r] can be merged in case "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sign"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1]) ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sign"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]) and they are not equal to zero."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"suffix  + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 и  - 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 can be merged with prefix  - 1 - 1 - 1 - 1"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"suffix  + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 can be merged with prefix  - 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 or prefix  + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 - 1"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Author's solution: "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/submission/22453451"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"http://codeforces.com/contest/739/submission/22453451"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"739D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"739D - Recover a functional graph"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's think what has to hold after we put numbers in place of question marks:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" number of vertices with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"precycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" should be divisible by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" if there exists a vertex with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"precycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then there should also exist a vertex with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"precycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cycle"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now looking at vertices without question marks, let's count for every cycle length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" how many vertices have to be added for condition 1 to hold (keeping in mind that this number cannot be 0 if there exist vertices of the form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") or (?, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) and which precycle lengths are missing for condition 2 to hold."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here it's important that for 2 we have to consider vertices of the form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", ?). Specifically, in order for everything to be univocally determined, it is necerrary to try all the cycle lengths for the vertex of this form with maximal "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Some contestants tried to assign cycle lengths for such vertices greedily and got WA#15."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's build the network for the flow which will have two parts. Vertices of the left part will correspond to the vertices of the functional graph that have at least one question mark. Vertices of the right part are the requirements of the form \"need "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" vertices of the form (0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")\" (for 1 to hold) and \"need one vertex of the from ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")\" (for 2 to hold). Add edges from the source to the left part, form the right part to the sink, and between requirements and vertices that satisfy them. If done correctly, with only one vertex created in the left part for all vertices of the same form in the functional graph, there will be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") nodes and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") edges in the network and the maximum flow will also be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Edmonds-Karp algorithm, which works in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"flow"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") will work in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") on this network. Accounting for the bruteforce of the cycle length for the maximal vertex of the form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", ?) we get "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") for the whole solution. In practice, any decent algorithm for finding maximum flow would most likely pass the system testing. Dinic works in 15 ms."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If all of the vertices in the right part are saturated - the answer is found. Rolling back the flow, we will find which vertices in the left part saturated which ones in the right part. Now we can change the question marks to numbers for those vertices in the left part, that were used, but some question marks might still remain. This is no big deal. Let's change all vertices of the form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", ?) to ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxc"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxc"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is that cycle length we bruteforce in the outermost loop. Instead of vertices of the form (?, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") we can write (1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Remember, that we created at least one cycle for all such "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s. Instead of (?, ?) simply put (0, 1)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now it's quite simple to get the answer to the problem. First create cycles out of the vertices of the form (0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), and then add edges ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") -> ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"739E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/739/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"739E - Gosha is hunting"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's divide Pokemons into 4 types: 0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"AB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" depending on Balls that we throw to them. Let's sort them by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in descending order. Let's iterate over last Pokemon in which we throw Ultra Ball (his type is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"AB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the index of this Pokemon. It is not hard to prove that there are no Pokemons to the left of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that have type 0 and all Pokemons to the right of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" have type 0 or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's sort all Pokemons to the left of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by (1 - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in descending order. Let's iterate last Pokemon of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"AB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be his index. We can prove that to the left of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" every Pokemon has type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"AB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (Let's call this group of Pokemons "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Between "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" every Pokemon has type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (will call them "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). To the right of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" only 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (will call them "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). We know that we throw an Ultra Ball to every Pokemon in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So let's add to our answer sum of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of all Pokemons in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Number of Pokemons in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to the difference between "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (number of Ultra Balls) and size of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Therefore we know number of Pokemons in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of type "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and we know how many Poke Balls are left for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" summarily. Not hard to prove that in group "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we should throw Poke Balls to Pokemons with greatest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Now we have to understand in which Pokemons in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we should throw Poke Balls. If we throw Poke Ball to Pokemon from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", it adds to the answer "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (1 - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - adds "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So we should throw Poke Balls to Pokemons with greatest values. When we iterate over "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", each iteration one Pokemon moves from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can keep structure that can add and delete one element, find minimum and keep sum of all elements in the structure. For example, treap or map (in c++). Let's keep 2 such structures: for calculating answer for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and for calculating answer for throwing Poke Balls to Pokemons in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The complexity - O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"logn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/48582"}